    "result_text": "All 11 test classes (Cp01\u2013Cp11) ran \u2014 21 tests, all green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe request-body flag `sharesHousehold` and the uniqueness bypass already existed. I added the missing piece: assigning and returning a stable shared `householdId`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `householdId` field mapped to a new `household_id` column, with getter/setter.\n\n2. **DB schemas** \u2014 added `household_id` to the `owners` table in all four schema files (`hsqldb` \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`).\n\n3. **OpenAPI** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a `readOnly` `householdId` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` returns it (mapped automatically by MapStruct). It stays absent from `OwnerFields`, so it remains response-only.\n\n4. **New pipeline step** `AssignHousehold` (`rest/function/owner/AssignHousehold.java`) \u2014 when `sharesHousehold` is true, it derives a stable identifier deterministically from the normalized `lastName`+`address` (same case-insensitive/whitespace-collapsed rule as `EnsureUniqueHousehold`), assigns it to the new owner, and backfills every already-stored household member so both sides of a join carry the same value. Requests without the flag leave `householdId` null.\n\n5. **Wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted `assignHousehold` between `build` and `assignCustomerCode`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nBecause the id is derived from the household key, all members compute the identical value (`householdId` is stable and shared by construction), satisfying the Cp11 requirement that co-household owners share a non-blank `householdId`. Cp10's existing 409/bypass behavior is untouched. Full suite: **21 tests, 0 failures**.",
